„­bible_version£KJV¤book:§chapter¨sections˜…¤book:§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙpJames, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, greeting.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book:§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙCMy brethren, count it all joy when ye fall into divers temptations;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ=knowing this, that the trying of your faith worketh patience.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ[But let patience have her perfect work, that ye may be perfect and entire, wanting nothing.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIf any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙGFor let not that man think that he shall receive any thing of the Lord.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ0A double minded man is unstable in all his ways.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book:§chapter«first_verse	ªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse	¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ<Let the brother of low degree rejoice in that he is exalted:¦layout ªlast_verse	„¥verse
¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ\but the rich, in that he is made low: because as the flower of the grass he shall pass away.¦layout ªlast_verse
„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÏFor the sun is no sooner risen with a burning heat, but it withereth the grass, and the flower thereof falleth, and the grace of the fashion of it perisheth: so also shall the rich man fade away in his ways.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book:§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙšBlessed is the man that endureth temptation: for when he is tried, he shall receive the crown of life, which the Lord hath promised to them that love him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙyLet no man say when he is tempted, I am tempted of God: for God cannot be tempted with evil, neither tempteth he any man:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙMbut every man is tempted, when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙiThen when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book:§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ Do not err, my beloved brethren.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ™Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, and cometh down from the Father of lights, with whom is no variableness, neither shadow of turning.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙmOf his own will begat he us with the word of truth, that we should be a kind of firstfruits of his creatures.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book:§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ]Wherefore, my beloved brethren, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ:for the wrath of man worketh not the righteousness of God.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’Wherefore lay apart all filthiness and superfluity of naughtiness, and receive with meekness the engrafted word, which is able to save your souls.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book:§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙMBut be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving your own selves.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙpFor if any be a hearer of the word, and not a doer, he is like unto a man beholding his natural face in a glass:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbfor he beholdeth himself, and goeth his way, and straightway forgetteth what manner of man he was.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙªBut whoso looketh into the perfect law of liberty, and continueth therein, he being not a forgetful hearer, but a doer of the work, this man shall be blessed in his deed.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book:§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIf any man among you seem to be religious, and bridleth not his tongue, but deceiveth his own heart, this man's religion is vain.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¤Pure religion and undefiled before God and the Father is this, To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction, and to keep himself unspotted from the world.¦layout ªlast_verse